From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps5cemu <ps5cemu@localhost>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 03:13:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PS5: bettervr.json's textureMemo keeps a texture unit's view
 while its SQ_TEX registers and the view cache are unchanged; built, untested
 on console

A sequence's first draw resolves every texture unit its shaders use through the view lookup cache
(LatteTextureViewLookupCache::lookup): the unit's SQ_TEX resource registers to a view, by scanning the
bucket the registers' address, size and pitch hash to. Breath of the Wild's stereo pass runs about
1,500 sequences a frame at 1.25x, each with its stages' units resolved again at every sequence, about
2 us of the record's sequence setup per sequence.

With the switch on, a unit's answer is kept per stage and unit: the seven SQ_TEX resource words as the
registers hold them, whether the shader samples the unit as depth, and the view it resolved to. A
sequence whose words are bit-identical, with no view added to or removed from the lookup cache since
the entry was made, binds that view and skips the scan; everything the words do not carry (the
texture's data hash, its swizzle and mip address, dynamic textures, the barrier check and the
effective scale) is still read from the texture on every sequence, as before, so a texture update or
invalidation needs no notice. The lookup cache's own changes are the one thing that can make the
answer wrong: Add and RemoveAll move a generation on, and an entry filled under an older one is not
used, so a destroyed view is never handed back.

What a hit skips, in the sequence setup: LatteTexture_ReconstructGX2Format, the dimension, pitch,
depth and height arithmetic, and the bucket scan of lookup or lookupWithColorOrDepthType. Estimated
0.5-1.2 ms off the record at 1.25x at 5-9k draws, from the sequence setup's share; the [draws] line's
"texture units reused" count shows how many units a frame answered from the memo.
